---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import ProductImage from "../components/ProductImage.astro";
import { productImage } from "../data/product-images";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";

const title = "Best Treadmills Over $2,500: When Premium Pricing Is Worth It";
const description =
  "We break down what separates a truly premium treadmill from an overpriced one, and which buyers actually benefit from spending over $2,500 on a home machine.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    id: "aviron-summit",
    name: "Aviron Summit Treadmill",
    category: "Premium Incline Trainer",
    badge: "Best Entry Point Over $2,500",
    amazonUrl:
      "https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0HGN8NNDK?tag=sktiger-20",
    pros: [
      "A -6% to 42% grade range, the steepest we have seen published for a home treadmill",
      "Hiking handles with speed, incline and heart-rate controls built in",
      "A 22 by 61-inch deck and a 4.5 CHP motor",
      "A choice of 16, 22 or 27-inch screens, and a 350 lb user rating",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Does not fold and weighs 364 lb",
      "Top speed falls as the grade rises: Aviron caps it at 6 mph above 25.5%",
      "Aviron recommends an 8 ft ceiling to use the full incline range",
      "Garage Gym Reviews notes owner reports that the larger screens can shake at higher speeds",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The Amazon-sold incline trainer for buyers who want an entirely different category of workout, steep hiking and downhill, not just a nicer flat treadmill.",
    description:
      "This slot used to hold the NordicTrack X22i Incline Trainer, which NordicTrack sells through its own store and retailers rather than on Amazon, so the card now shows the incline trainer Amazon does sell that comes closest to it. The Summit is Aviron’s incline trainer, and like the X22i it turns a treadmill into a hiking and mountain-terrain machine rather than a flat running surface with a tilt. Aviron, Garage Gym Reviews and Wellworthy agree on a 4.5 CHP motor, a -6% to 42% grade range and a 22 by 61-inch belt, and Aviron and Garage Gym Reviews agree on a 350 lb user limit, a 364 lb machine weight and dimensions of 67 by 37 by 71 inches; it does not fold and moves on transport wheels. Aviron gives a 14 mph top speed but limits it by grade, capping it at 6 mph above 25.5%, and sources disagree on the exact top speed, so we have not printed one. Aviron recommends an 8 ft ceiling and users up to 6 ft 8 in. On 20 October 2026 Aviron listed it at $2,999 (regular $3,999) with the 16-inch screen, $3,298 with the 22-inch and $3,597 with the 27-inch, and on 9 November 2026 its Amazon listing showed <strong>$2,849</strong> with the 16-inch screen and <strong>$3,148</strong> with the 22-inch. We have not tested it ourselves; our <a href=\"/aviron-treadmill\">Aviron treadmill guide</a> covers the range, and our <a href=\"/nordictrack-x22i-incline-trainer-treadmill\">X22i review</a> covers the machine it replaces.",
  },
  {
    id: "life-fitness-t3",
    name: "Life Fitness T3",
    editorialScore: 4.7,
    category: "Club-Grade Home Treadmill",
    badge: "Best Club-Quality Feel",
    amazonUrl:
      "https://www.amazon.com/dp/B006BE6HW4?tag=sktiger-20",
    pros: [
      "FlexDeck shock absorption system used in actual commercial gyms",
      "Commercial-derived frame built to resist flex at high speeds",
      "Simple, durable console avoids unnecessary subscription lock-in",
      "Drive system derived from commercial gym equipment",
      "Reputation for multi-decade reliability in commercial settings",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Console feels basic next to giant touchscreen competitors",
      "Premium price for relatively minimal software features",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "If you've ever run on a treadmill at a nice gym and wondered why your home machine doesn't feel the same, the T3 is that same commercial DNA scaled for home use.",
    description:
      "Life Fitness built its reputation supplying treadmills to commercial gyms and hotel fitness centers, and the T3 is the closest most home buyers can get to that exact experience without buying literal club equipment. The standout feature is the FlexDeck shock absorption system, the same underlying technology used in Life Fitness's commercial-grade machines, which uses a suspension design rather than simple foam padding to absorb impact. A suspension deck like this is designed to be smoother at footstrike and more stable through the stride, particularly at faster paces where cheaper decks tend to feel busier underfoot. Frame construction is the other reason to buy it: a frame derived from equipment built to survive years of continuous commercial use is, as a general tendency, far less prone to side-to-side flex or console wobble than a home-tier frame. We have not measured its noise or flex ourselves. Where the T3 differs from most premium competitors is its console: it's simple and functional rather than a giant touchscreen, with no subscription ecosystem to speak of, which some buyers will see as a drawback and others will see as refreshingly straightforward. Typically priced between <strong>$3,499 and $3,999</strong>, it's a genuine investment, but it's aimed squarely at buyers who care more about ride quality and long-term durability than software features. Read our full <a href=\"/life-fitness-t3-treadmill\">Life Fitness T3 review</a> for the complete breakdown.",
  },
  {
    id: "peloton-tread-vision",
    name: "Peloton Tread Vision",
    category: "Flagship Interactive Treadmill",
    badge: "Best Interactive Flagship",
    amazonUrl:
      "https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0HDRFM27D?tag=sktiger-20",
    pros: [
      "Peloton's instructor-led running, walking and strength classes, which are the platform's main draw",
      "A 23.8-inch screen that swivels for off-belt strength and yoga classes",
      "Movement-tracking camera for running-form and rep feedback, new on this generation",
      "Speed and incline knobs with jump buttons for instant interval changes",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Launched on 1 October 2026, so there is no long-term independent coverage of reliability yet",
      "The form-tracking, personalised plans and classes are membership features, and the membership is ongoing",
      "Does not fold, and its 59-inch belt is a little shorter than the 60 inches on most machines here",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The interactive flagship Amazon sells: for someone who already knows they want Peloton classes and will keep the All-Access Membership.",
    description:
      "This slot used to hold the NordicTrack Commercial 2950, which NordicTrack sells through its own store and retailers rather than on Amazon. NordicTrack's flagships on Amazon sit below this guide's $2,500 floor, so the card now shows the interactive flagship Amazon does sell in this price tier. Peloton replaced its treadmill line in autumn 2026 with three machines, and the Tread Vision is the one most buyers will consider. Peloton's specification page and its launch material agree on a 23.8-inch swivel touchscreen, 0 to 12.5 mph and 0 to 12.5% incline, and the machine does not fold; Peloton lists a 59 by 20-inch cushioned belt and a 300 lb maximum user weight. Its new feature is a movement-tracking camera that scores running efficiency from cadence and vertical oscillation and counts reps in strength classes. As with the 2950 and iFIT, the content is the point: Peloton prices the hardware with the All-Access Membership sold separately and says the membership is required for its content and features, though its shop page also describes a Just Run mode for running without a class. Against the 2950 you give up decline and some incline. Because the machine has only been on sale since 1 October, we cannot point to independent reliability coverage. Typical pricing is <strong>around $3,500</strong>. We have not tested it ourselves; see our <a href=\"/peloton-tread\">Peloton Tread guide</a>.",
  },
  {
    id: "life-fitness-run-cx",
    name: "Life Fitness Run CX",
    category: "Gym-Pedigree Home Treadmill",
    badge: "Closest Club Series Alternative on Amazon",
    amazonUrl:
      "https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0C6B3C6FV?tag=sktiger-20",
    pros: [
      "Life Fitness’s FlexDeck shock absorption system, a name it uses on several of its treadmills",
      "Lifetime cover on the frame and on the deck shock absorbers, according to a retailer and an independent review",
      "A 400 lb maximum user weight and a 10-year motor warranty",
      "A low 6-inch step-up height, stated by Life Fitness and by retailers",
    ],
    cons: [
      "A home-line machine, not the club-floor hardware of the Platinum Club Series",
      "Does not fold",
      "An independent review describes the deck as firm rather than soft, and the button interface as awkward",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The Life Fitness to buy on Amazon if you want gym-style construction and a long warranty on the cushioning itself, for a fraction of Club Series money.",
    description:
      "This slot used to hold the Life Fitness Platinum Club Series, the club-floor treadmill Life Fitness sells through its commercial channels and this page priced at $11,999 to $15,999, which Amazon does not sell new. The card now shows the closest Life Fitness machine Amazon does sell, at about a third of the price. The Run CX is the home version of a gym-pedigree design, and its cushioning comes from Life Fitness's FlexDeck Shock Absorption System. A specialist retailer lists lifetime cover on the frame and on the \"Lifesprings\", the deck's shock absorbers, alongside a 10-year motor, 7 years on parts and 1 year of labour, and an independent review gives the same structure. Retailers and reviewers agree on a 22 by 60-inch deck, 0 to 15% incline, a 12 mph top speed, a 400 lb maximum user weight and a non-folding frame, and Life Fitness lists a 6-inch step-up. What it is not is the Platinum Club Series: it is built for heavy home use rather than a busy gym floor, and one independent review describes the deck as firm but adequate rather than plush. Typical pricing is <strong>$4,300-$4,900</strong>. We have not tested it ourselves; read our <a href=\"/life-fitness-run-cx-treadmill\">Life Fitness Run CX review</a>.",
  },
  {
    id: "matrix-t7xe",
    name: "Matrix Fitness T7xe",
    editorialScore: 4.8,
    category: "Commercial-Grade Home Treadmill",
    badge: "Best True Commercial-Grade",
    amazonUrl:
      "https://www.amazon.com/dp/B00ANUECL0?tag=sktiger-20",
    pros: [
      "Genuinely commercial-rated frame, not a home unit dressed up as one",
      "Massive weight capacity and a commercial frame built to resist flex",
      "16-inch console with intuitive, uncluttered interface",
      "Adjustable cushioning system tuned by Matrix's commercial engineering team",
      "Reputation for surviving heavy daily use in actual gym settings",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Among the priciest machines in this guide by a wide margin",
      "Overkill for anyone not running frequently or at high volume",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The T7xe is genuinely commercial equipment sold for home use, appropriate for serious runners but overkill for casual walkers.",
    description:
      "Matrix Fitness sells the T7xe to actual commercial gyms as standard equipment, and the version available for home purchase is, unlike some \"commercial-inspired\" home machines, the same underlying hardware rather than a home-tier frame with commercial branding. That distinction is the whole case for it: a commercial frame is, as a general tendency, far less prone to flex during hard sprint intervals at higher body weights than a home-tier frame, and that holds even against the other premium picks in this guide. Matrix's cushioning system, tuned by the same engineering team responsible for their gym-floor equipment, aims at a deliberately firm-but-forgiving balance designed for runners logging serious mileage rather than occasional walkers. The 16-inch console is refreshingly uncluttered, prioritizing clear metrics over flashy graphics, which suits buyers who find software-heavy competitors distracting. Weight capacity and motor durability are both rated for continuous commercial use, meaning the specs printed on the box reflect actual sustained daily operation rather than occasional home sessions. We have not tested it ourselves. All of this comes at a real cost: typically <strong>$4,999 to $5,999</strong>, making it one of the most expensive machines we cover anywhere on this site. For serious, high-mileage runners, we think it's genuinely worth it. For casual walkers or occasional joggers, it's considerably more machine, and more money, than the use case calls for. Read our full <a href=\"/matrix-fitness-t7xe-treadmill\">Matrix Fitness T7xe review</a> for the complete breakdown.",
  },
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/best-of",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Treadmills Over $2,500",
          item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/treadmills-over-2500",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is a treadmill over $2,500 actually worth it for most home users?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "For most casual walkers and occasional joggers, no. The jump in this tier mainly benefits frequent runners logging significant weekly mileage, buyers who specifically want extreme incline training, or households where multiple people put heavy, sustained wear on the machine. If you run under 10-15 miles a week and don't need incline training beyond a standard range, a $1,500-$2,000 machine likely covers your needs just as well.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What specifically justifies spending over $2,500 on a treadmill?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Three things justify the price on paper: motor and frame durability rated for genuinely heavy, sustained use rather than occasional sessions; incline ranges that go meaningfully beyond a standard treadmill, like the Aviron Summit's 42% maximum grade; and ride quality refinements, like Life Fitness's FlexDeck system, that matter most to frequent, higher-mileage runners rather than casual users.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Do premium treadmills still require an iFit or similar subscription?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "It depends on the brand. NordicTrack's premium machines remain iFit-dependent for most of their standout features regardless of price, and the Peloton Tread Vision's classes and form tracking need Peloton's All-Access Membership. Life Fitness and Matrix Fitness machines in this tier generally do not require a subscription for core functionality, reflecting their commercial-equipment heritage where subscription models are less standard.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "When is spending this much overkill?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "If your workouts are primarily walking, if you use the treadmill a few times a week rather than daily, or if you don't have a strong preference around incline training or ride refinement, you're very likely to be just as satisfied with a well-reviewed machine in the $1,500-$2,500 range. Spending more in that scenario buys durability headroom you won't need and features you won't use.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How much more durable are these machines really?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Meaningfully more, particularly for machines like the Matrix T7xe that use genuinely commercial-rated components rather than home-tier parts. These machines are engineered and tested for continuous multi-hour daily use in gym settings, which translates to a substantial durability buffer for home use, even heavy home use, well beyond what standard home treadmills are built to withstand.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Should I buy new or consider a certified refurbished commercial unit at this price?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "It's worth researching both. Certified refurbished commercial treadmills from reputable dealers can sometimes deliver similar hardware to what's covered in this guide at a lower price, though warranty coverage and condition vary significantly by seller, so we'd only recommend that route through an established, reputable refurbisher rather than an unverified private sale.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: { "@type": "Person", "@id": "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/editorial-standards/#shaheer-ali-khan", name: "Shaheer Ali Khan", honorificSuffix: "PhD", jobTitle: "Exercise Physiology & Biomechanics Specialist", url: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/editorial-standards/#who", sameAs: ["https://www.linkedin.com/in/shaheer-ali-khan-data/"], worksFor: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" } },
      datePublished: "2026-06-10",
      dateModified: "2026-06-10",
      publisher: {
        "@type": "Organization",
        name: "Treadmill Reviews USA",
      },
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      image: productImage(product.name)?.url,
      description: product.description,
      brand: { "@type": "Brand", name: product.name.split(" ")[0] },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={`${title} | Treadmill Reviews USA`}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: "2026-06-10",
    modifiedTime: "2026-06-10",
    author: "Shaheer Ali Khan",
    section: "Fitness Equipment",
    tags: [
      "premium treadmill",
      "commercial treadmill",
      "high-end treadmill",
      "treadmill buying guide",
      "home gym",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{ text: "See Top Picks", link: "#top-picks" }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden legacy-article">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <svg
        class="absolute inset-0 w-full h-full opacity-10"
        xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
      >
        <pattern
          id="dot-pattern"
          x="0"
          y="0"
          width="24"
          height="24"
          patternUnits="userSpaceOnUse"
        >
          <circle cx="2" cy="2" r="1.5" fill="white"></circle>
        </pattern>
        <rect width="100%" height="100%" fill="url(#dot-pattern)"></rect>
      </svg>
      <div
        class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center"
      >
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex items-center justify-center gap-3 mb-6 flex-wrap">
          <span
            class="bg-[#FF5A1F] text-white px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest"
          >
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span
            class="bg-green-500 text-white px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest"
          >
            Updated June 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          Best <span class="text-[#5AA9FF]">Treadmills Over $2,500</span>: When Premium Pricing Is Worth It
        </h1>
        <p
          class="text-xl text-gray-300 max-w-2xl mx-auto leading-relaxed"
        >
          Five machines that justify their price tag, and an honest
          breakdown of exactly which buyers actually benefit from spending
          this much on a home treadmill.
        </p>
        <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-4">
          <a
            href="#top-picks"
            class="bg-[#FF5A1F] hover:bg-[#E64F16] text-white px-8 py-4 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm"
          >
            See Top Picks
          </a>
          <a
            href="#worth-it"
            class="bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white px-8 py-4 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm border border-white/20"
          >
            Is It Worth It?
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div
        class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-4 py-8 border-b border-gray-100"
      >
        <img
          src="/authors/shaheer-ali-khan.png"
          alt="Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD"
          width="48" height="48" class="w-12 h-12 rounded-full"
        />
        <div>
          <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 leading-tight"><a href="/editorial-standards/#who" rel="author" class="text-gray-900 no-underline hover:underline">Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD</a></p>
          <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm leading-tight">
            Exercise Physiology &amp; Biomechanics Specialist · ACSM-EP, CSCS
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block w-px h-8 bg-gray-200"></div>
        <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm">
          Last Updated: <strong class="text-gray-700">June 10, 2026</strong>
        </p>
        <div class="ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="3.4k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
        <h2 class="flex items-center gap-2 text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mt-0 mb-4">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="w-5 h-5 text-[#FF5A1F]" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M4 6h16M4 12h16M4 18h7"></path></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
            <a href="#worth-it" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">1. Is Spending Over $2,500 Actually Worth It?</a>
            <a href="#what-changes" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">2. What Actually Changes at This Price Point</a>
            <a href="#top-picks" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">3. Five Premium Treadmills Worth the Money</a>
            <a href="#who-should-buy" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">4. Who Should (and Shouldn't) Shop This Tier</a>
            <a href="#buying-considerations" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">5. Delivery, Installation, and Other Costs to Expect</a>
            <a href="#faq" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">6. Frequently Asked Questions</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">

      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <!-- Lede -->
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          There's a version of this guide that just lists expensive
          treadmills and calls it a day. That's not this guide. Spending
          over $2,500 on a treadmill is a real financial decision, and for
          a meaningful share of buyers, it's the wrong one, not because
          these machines aren't excellent, but because their strengths
          don't match every household's actual usage pattern. We compared five machines that genuinely earn their price tags, and we're
          going to be honest throughout about exactly who benefits from
          spending this much and who would be better served saving the
          difference.
        </p>

        <!-- SECTION 1 -->
        <div id="worth-it" class="scroll-mt-24">
        <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. Is Spending Over $2,500 Actually Worth It?</h2>
        <p>
          The honest answer is: it depends heavily on how you'll actually
          use the machine, and we'd rather tell you that upfront than
          spend this whole guide pretending every reader needs a
          commercial-grade treadmill. If your exercise routine consists of
          walking a few times a week, or jogging occasional easy miles,
          the returns on spending $2,500-plus diminish quickly, and a
          well-chosen machine in the $1,500-$2,000 range will likely serve
          you just as well for years.
        </p>
        <p>
          Where this tier genuinely earns its price is with buyers whose
          usage pattern would stress a standard home treadmill over time.
          That includes runners logging 15-plus miles a week, households
          where the treadmill sees near-daily use from multiple people,
          anyone training specifically for incline-heavy events like trail
          races or <a href="/best-weighted-vests-for-walking/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>rucking</a>, and buyers who simply want the smoothest,
          quietest, most refined ride quality available and have the
          budget to prioritize it without financial strain.
        </p>
        <p>
          It also matters how you weigh durability against features. Some
          machines in this tier, like the Matrix T7xe and Life Fitness T3,
          justify their price primarily through frame engineering and
          motor durability rated for genuinely heavy use, with relatively
          modest software features. Others, like the Peloton Tread Vision
          and the Aviron Summit, justify their price through screen size, incline
          range, and interactive coaching, with durability that's strong
          but not dramatically beyond what you'd find a tier down. Knowing
          which kind of value you're actually paying for helps you avoid
          spending on the wrong strength for your needs.
        </p>
        <p>
          There's also a psychological dimension worth naming honestly.
          Expensive equipment can create pressure to use it more, which
          sounds like a benefit but can backfire if it becomes a source of
          guilt rather than motivation. We'd encourage buyers considering
          this tier to be honest about their actual exercise consistency
          over the past year, not their aspirational routine, since a
          $4,000 machine used twice a month delivers worse value than a
          $1,200 machine used four times a week.
        </p>

        <div class="not-prose bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8 my-8">
          <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-6 text-center">
            <div>
              <p class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-black text-[#5AA9FF]">
                4.0-4.25
              </p>
              <p class="text-xs text-gray-400 mt-1 uppercase tracking-wide">
                Typical Motor CHP
              </p>
            </div>
            <div>
              <p class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-black text-[#5AA9FF]">
                350-400
              </p>
              <p class="text-xs text-gray-400 mt-1 uppercase tracking-wide">
                Weight Capacity (lbs.)
              </p>
            </div>
            <div>
              <p class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-black text-[#5AA9FF]">
                Up to 40%
              </p>
              <p class="text-xs text-gray-400 mt-1 uppercase tracking-wide">
                Max Incline (Select Models)
              </p>
            </div>
            <div>
              <p class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-black text-[#5AA9FF]">
                $2,999-$15,999
              </p>
              <p class="text-xs text-gray-400 mt-1 uppercase tracking-wide">
                Price Range Covered
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

