          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">
              What specifically justifies spending over $2,500 on a treadmill?
            </h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
              Three things justify the price on paper: motor and frame durability rated for genuinely heavy, sustained use rather than occasional sessions;
              incline ranges that go meaningfully beyond a standard
              treadmill, like the Aviron Summit's <strong>42% maximum grade</strong>;
              and ride quality refinements, like Life Fitness's FlexDeck system, that matter most to frequent, higher-mileage runners rather than casual users.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">
              Do premium treadmills still require an iFit or similar subscription?
            </h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
              It depends on the brand. NordicTrack's premium machines
              remain iFit-dependent for most of their standout features
              regardless of price, and the Peloton Tread Vision's classes
              and form tracking need Peloton's All-Access Membership. Life Fitness and Matrix Fitness
              machines in this tier generally do not require a
              subscription for core functionality, reflecting their
              commercial-equipment heritage where subscription models are
              less standard.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">
              When is spending this much overkill?
            </h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
              If your workouts are primarily walking, if you use the
              treadmill a few times a week rather than daily, or if you
              don't have a strong preference around incline training or
              ride refinement, you're very likely to be just as satisfied
              with a well-reviewed machine in the $1,500-$2,500 range.
              Spending more in that scenario buys durability headroom you
              won't need and features you won't use.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">
              How much more durable are these machines really?
            </h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
              Meaningfully more, particularly for machines like the Matrix
              T7xe that use
              genuinely commercial-rated components rather than home-tier
